.TH ntptime 8
.SH NAME
ntptime - read and set kernel time variables
.SH \ 

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBntptime [ -chr ] [ -e \fIest_error\fB ] [ -f \fIfrequency\fB ] [ -m \fImax_error\fB ] [ -o \fIoffset\fB ] [ -s \fIstatus\fB ] [ -t \fItime_constant\fB]\fR 

.SH DESCRIPTION

This program is useful only with special kernels described in the A Kernel Model for Precision Timekeeping page. It reads and displays time-related kernel variables using the \fBntp_gettime()\fR system call. A similar display can be obtained using the \fBntpdc\fR program and \fBkerninfo\fR command.

.SH OPTIONS

.RS 0
.TP 8
\fB-c\fR
Display the execution time of \fBntptime\fR itself.
.TP 8
\fB-e \fIest_error\fB\fR
Specify estimated error, in microseconds.
.TP 8
\fB-f \fIfrequency\fB\fR
Specify frequency offset, in parts per million.
.TP 8
\fB-h\fR
Display help information.
.TP 8
\fB-m \fImax_error\fB\fR
Specify max possible errors, in microseconds.
.TP 8
\fB-o \fIoffset\fB\fR
Specify clock offset, in microseconds.
.TP 8
\fB-r\fR
Display Unix and NTP times in raw format.
.TP 8
\fB-s \fIstatus\fB\fR
Specify clock status. Better know what you are doing.
.TP 8
\fB-t \fItime_constant\fB\fR
Specify time constant, an integer in the range 0-10. 
.RE

